#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615407 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d46288-0093-5e1f-9131-6c8e56a1edd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(C)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186614734} {:answer/id #uuid "3c8e0aae-4af3-57f4-a548-b99e3ea7c679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186614838} {:answer/id #uuid "9b188aff-52e4-5f1c-bc9d-984eee97a783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186615016} {:answer/id #uuid "af510952-4d77-51f5-9210-94048341bbfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186615056} {:answer/id #uuid "cf309abb-2e0e-5041-859e-8117d22f9b04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186615114}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615114} :answer-field/id #uuid "6581a51e-d2ac-5c68-a58c-7d87460939e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186615263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7db38a79-5d22-508b-95d6-b09b834439e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-75091" :question/problem "The matrix $C$ is given below. Let ${c}_{1}$, ${c}_{2}$,  ${c}_{3}$, and ${c}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $C.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(C).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${c}_{4}={x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -7 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $C$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -7 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(C)$ is $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${c}_{4}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{c}_{4}}=\\underbrace{(-3)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{c}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(2)}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{c}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-3)\\cdot 2=-6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615410 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "127d2b23-75ad-5393-995d-b95237ed095f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186614757} {:answer/id #uuid "2984cb2b-f04f-5bc0-974d-9adb3e91839b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186614798} {:answer/id #uuid "4391fac7-95ce-5c2a-94d8-9eb02d73a501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186614852} {:answer/id #uuid "960bfd0e-b425-55a4-be8f-1c036917da9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186615004} {:answer/id #uuid "aef70815-b913-545b-b6dc-bbdf28e44da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186615055}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615055} :answer-field/id #uuid "e04854eb-e91c-5025-adaa-6570ce13e9bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186615333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7fa9fb28-d679-5782-b792-a3d05e943fd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-111191" :question/problem "Consider the matrix \n$H=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 11 & -9 \\\\ 4 & 21 & -15\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(H).$" :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(H),$ we reduce the matrix $H$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 11 & -9 \\\\ 4 & 21 & -15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 21 & -15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(H)$ is $\\{{h}_{1},{h}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e2acba3-ba0a-58f5-9d98-f3e4f1fa253f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -24\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186614746} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb82341-70c7-55ac-8562-abf7bf759db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186614806} {:answer/id #uuid "59d78011-c65f-5175-83cf-29368190bc92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -24\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186614898} {:answer/id #uuid "5a01f0f5-7bc3-55b4-b34d-76b4d623e97d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186614899} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb3038b-d0fe-5396-af1b-ac162557e6ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186615159}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186614899} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce348764-50f6-5b18-85e8-ddf9c07483f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186615323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aeac3161-d828-5722-838e-c65d1de142c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-111186" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$Z=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 15 & -24\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(Z).$" :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(Z),$ we reduce the matrix $Z$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 15 & -24\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${z}_{1},$ and ${z}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Z)$ is $\\{{z}_{1},{z}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18032349-8bb4-508a-82ee-a0f1122513b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186615606} {:answer/id #uuid "65bddc3b-3e50-5594-9340-55f2f6b9a62c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186615747} {:answer/id #uuid "93cef4a9-e252-55c0-917a-bf6af7cfc386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186615841} {:answer/id #uuid "98c67c9c-e918-5007-b109-58515c4855b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186615848} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ce0576-4196-5c88-a63b-727b78f8674d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186615917}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615841} :answer-field/id #uuid "54595b25-a2e4-5c8d-8f4c-15145de850fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "edf0b013-94e4-5bd1-84aa-1e5d64b8c509" :question/math-academy-id "q-34971" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $5\\times 8$ matrix that has $5$ **non-pivot** columns, then $\\text{dim(Null}(C))=$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\text{dim(Null}(C))$ is the number of non-pivot columns in $C.$\n\nSince we are told that $C$ has $5$ non-pivot columns, we have \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(C))=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616956 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11608109-63f4-5381-863e-6e92792cc660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616410} {:answer/id #uuid "70ead1a7-c4f0-5c8f-b43c-6dc1d4b49e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616574} {:answer/id #uuid "8d64e571-5d10-561a-ab42-498565eee28f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616623} {:answer/id #uuid "c9871779-87ac-5527-9f77-c162b9fc5de2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616740} {:answer/id #uuid "dbcdff5c-fc94-5c25-ad88-bd265bf3a798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616768}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616410} :answer-field/id #uuid "a65d4c6c-16b6-5e9e-beb1-c18412078827" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2dc2fed3-d655-5205-951d-af8d7bb52e4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-142473" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and  $det(A)=0?$ \n\nI. $A$ is invertible\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}$\n\nIII. $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col)}(A)=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null)}(A)=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. A matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=0,$ our matrix $A$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $A$ is *not* invertible, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible,  it must be $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ffa830c-d80c-50f1-a9d5-fe1ebcd7331a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616472} {:answer/id #uuid "74e42cb9-e4af-5881-b608-c6f11cb10b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616580} {:answer/id #uuid "a7630b66-b242-57a5-9ee0-cb303aa7fd5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616679} {:answer/id #uuid "b0012022-5d79-54b1-9714-0e41233affc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616695} {:answer/id #uuid "ffae7155-96c2-535c-bde8-3a20e9b83925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616821}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616695} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a148ab1-c122-59b6-a4a9-dfb8bdba9c0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c53dbaf-3ce4-5d86-a287-de9fed2a2135" :question/math-academy-id "q-141193" :question/problem "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border:2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${R}^{n}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04c50643-01aa-57fa-8703-551f411bcef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616382} {:answer/id #uuid "341e787a-6216-56d1-addd-83742093725f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616459} {:answer/id #uuid "bc33d81e-9442-5b3d-9e29-5ebf4f6366a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616713} {:answer/id #uuid "d5396c66-36ca-5609-9c8e-2f99af1e2e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616758} {:answer/id #uuid "e3317a2c-6551-5c55-9f93-e3277b68058b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616775}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616775} :answer-field/id #uuid "48d03ec9-bb33-5a1b-9a2d-866361f6881e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a20e0f6e-9a4b-59f0-b28f-3a10b5209120" :question/math-academy-id "q-141205" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $A$ is invertible\n\nII. All columns of $A$ are pivot columns\n\nIII. $Ax=0$ has no non-trivial solution" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then $A$ is invertible.* $\\,✓$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the matrix $A$ must be invertible.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=n,$ which means that all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then $Ax=0$ has no non-trivial solution.* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\},$ which means that $Ax=0$ has no non-trivial solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617034 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02717221-4f50-5e06-99ab-b59d6c370818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616376} {:answer/id #uuid "4362c2da-1b14-5a05-82ec-cd9d02715c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616481} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e5b857-ddf6-57fa-9bba-e751dd4041cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616681} {:answer/id #uuid "c3aeddf3-6a3f-5940-a84a-a0d812268bed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616728} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ca4f0f-0cea-584c-9154-5654b6b76388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616781}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616681} :answer-field/id #uuid "2903c293-1eda-5011-b735-91c9fbb84274" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f35a0916-c2f9-5592-8918-2772549e2c23" :question/math-academy-id "q-141197" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and  $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5}?$ \n\nI. $A$ is invertible\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(A)=6$\n\nIII. $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. By the invertible matrix theorem,  $A$ is invertible because $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since,\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{rank}(A)=\\text{dim(Col}(A))=5.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is invertible,  it must be $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e94a9de-dfcb-5b50-aac5-2e72e6992c8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617335} {:answer/id #uuid "42ce0c2d-171a-5820-b84e-efb541e4a8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617373} {:answer/id #uuid "7b6e85bb-b725-5fa1-a3f0-5d33b2926f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617452} {:answer/id #uuid "7ebcf029-a439-5a54-8ed4-ce88c7665766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617456} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9bc25a-e906-54f9-807a-952ffad9cee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617587}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617373} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef98946f-15ca-573e-9bc3-760d0d2745ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186617757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad5d2e27-8fc3-540c-95a0-f40ed1326296" :question/math-academy-id "q-83717" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\nI. $U=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle V=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle W=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\},$ \n$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ and \n$\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,$ $V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0,\\,{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\\,{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0,\\,{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0,\\,{w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=12-12=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- The set $V$ cannot be orthogonal since it contains $3$ vectors from ${R}^{2}$ (in which we can have an orthogonal set with at most $2$ non-zero vectors). Therefore, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=8+0-2=6\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186626698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0129cd62-aaee-5202-aa0f-f7e649fb5481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186626095} {:answer/id #uuid "32205684-ff99-5bfd-b346-2b4200da3283" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186626167} {:answer/id #uuid "62d50961-ed6a-59cb-9bbe-be735e2243f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{p}_{1},{p}_{3}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(P)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186626253} {:answer/id #uuid "6fda2fd5-f944-5c02-b857-95931c1dd7d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186626282} {:answer/id #uuid "73a1e3e4-8189-54a9-998e-71cf44a1c393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186626287}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186626287} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cb16bf2-396e-50bb-a4d2-d0875772495c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186626602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3bc90585-165f-505d-9c04-7bc57e1bcc21" :question/math-academy-id "q-75959" :question/problem "The matrix $P$ is given below. Let ${p}_{1}$, ${p}_{2}$, and ${p}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $P.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{p}_{1},{p}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(P).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${p}_{2}={x}_{1}{p}_{1}+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}.$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $P$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(P)$ is $\\{{p}_{1},{p}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${p}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{{p}_{2}}=\\underbrace{(3)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{p}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(0)}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{p}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=3\\cdot 0=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186626715 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "990cccfc-aa22-5e9a-8d6f-0f706c5f9ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186626348} {:answer/id #uuid "990ead01-4200-561e-9670-0ad813576c5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186626349} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d3342f-122f-51d5-8919-c4198ed8c089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{f}_{1},{f}_{2}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186626448} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2ac866-b342-5a6b-9cfe-e84d8ff5ebcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186626464} {:answer/id #uuid "deb27185-d53d-5080-a215-e6357b2a1054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186626491}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186626349} :answer-field/id #uuid "5754f418-c270-5874-af4d-e1907b917433" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186626598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6619c8cc-4708-50a5-b348-67021e5a70e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-35683" :question/problem "The matrix $F$ is given below. Let ${f}_{1}$, ${f}_{2}$,  ${f}_{3}$, and ${f}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $F.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{f}_{1},{f}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(F).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${f}_{3}={x}_{1}{f}_{1}+{x}_{2}{f}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nF=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $F$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$ is $\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${f}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{f}_{3}}=\\underbrace{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{f}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(-1)}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{f}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-1)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186626778 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58e3015a-0e6a-599d-9850-5dd33effc6ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186626234} {:answer/id #uuid "881bbdc9-ec69-50fb-9661-d6af87989c96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186626323} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a9dda8-c489-5e16-92d0-bd95e0311497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186626382} {:answer/id #uuid "d286bda4-62b9-5e78-95ea-c81688c4c13c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186626476} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1361fc-7621-5d10-96bc-54eaa976d87c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186626539}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186626382} :answer-field/id #uuid "c54895e1-41d0-5492-a248-1c4eb8e54ff9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186626642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0900510-1fc1-598d-94cc-b26a352b6f93" :question/math-academy-id "q-75071" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$Z=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(Z).$" :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(Z),$ we reduce the matrix $Z$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st, $2$nd and $3$rd columns are pivot columns.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st, $2$nd and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${z}_{1},$ ${z}_{2},$ and ${z}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Z)$ is $\\{{z}_{1},{z}_{2},{z}_{3}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186627687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "217d00e3-6085-5217-9e0f-bc0ed267a659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186627152} {:answer/id #uuid "4d216911-9fdd-5d94-9372-99c99ec03e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186627226} {:answer/id #uuid "c69e47ba-4ae4-5ccc-94e3-da31c8bee3bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186627431} {:answer/id #uuid "cb083657-c6c3-5e24-8fd7-4d2f8e37c426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186627440} {:answer/id #uuid "d61c0f82-de33-5eb0-80d0-e768ca91585e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186627460}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186627440} :answer-field/id #uuid "5be541ef-8f3c-5ccd-a814-640b71ca741c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186627583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48e66ecf-d81b-50c1-a8a1-a9e2ad76a682" :question/math-academy-id "q-141217" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{nullity}(A)=0$\n\nII. $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}$\n\nIII. $det(A)=0$" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ then  $\\text{nullity}(A)=0.$* $\\,\\times$\n\nBy the invertible matrix theorem, if  $\\text{Col}(A)$ is not ${R}^{n}$ then  $A$ is not an invertible matrix and  so we must have $\\text{nullity}(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ then  $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}.$* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, by the invertible matrix theorem, if  $\\text{Col}(A)$ is not ${R}^{n}$ then  $A$ is not an invertible matrix and  so we must have $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ then  $det(A)=0.$* $\\,✓$  \n\nSince $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is not invertible, which means $det(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186627692 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e0fc9f-7385-5c7b-ae3b-103b9330e3c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186627091} {:answer/id #uuid "1c378646-9302-5a64-9728-c1b51529df79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186627145} {:answer/id #uuid "708def97-75ed-58ef-a2bf-6861c83ab912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186627282} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8fd432-6f8f-51c7-bfbb-559a7ea21b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186627354} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c36613-e9d3-5ba7-91ca-a44714c54624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186627371}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186627091} :answer-field/id #uuid "48b9389d-612d-5d40-ba4a-42e4e4c73b9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186627573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "624ebabe-0dfa-5173-b265-5c678170209c" :question/math-academy-id "q-142478" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix,  then $\\text{__________}$ .*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}$\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n\nIII. $Ax=0$ has only the trivial solution" :question/worked-solution "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=3=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix,  then  $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}.$* $\\,\\times$ \n\nSince $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem,  we have $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix,  then $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem,  we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=3=\\text{rank}(A).$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix,  then $Ax=0$ has only the trivial solution.* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\},$ which means that $Ax=0$ has only the trivial solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186627715 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2db7a114-79ca-5bba-8124-a7181493b13b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{rank}(A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186627175} {:answer/id #uuid "3d068235-7187-58b5-8e9a-5227645c538c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Col}(A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186627198} {:answer/id #uuid "5962a640-dcc7-5027-bb07-b17fe801639b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{dim(Col}(A))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186627245} {:answer/id #uuid "988e139f-a77f-5b44-b4d1-f1beefdeed76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Null}(A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186627350} {:answer/id #uuid "a129ec1b-a9f3-52d1-9974-a86f524b2b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{nullity}(A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186627363}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186627363} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d11f370-8031-53e9-948e-0abb973068db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186627571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a697d815-4993-501b-8fbd-3b051df82a6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-141195" :question/problem "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\bbox[3pt,white,border:2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\text{nullity}(A).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186630982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a65548e-2b88-561b-8fc0-6a2b1c002a25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186630478} {:answer/id #uuid "5fdd8b14-0a1f-5a88-8f2c-cc1895c13a77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186630571} {:answer/id #uuid "721776a8-8af8-5ccd-978b-fc269335557b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186630596} {:answer/id #uuid "81b37717-1d3e-5d3f-85c9-5462cb83b193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186630620} {:answer/id #uuid "a29c2ac1-2972-53a7-bab9-643d8eb1389b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186630677}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186630478} :answer-field/id #uuid "afafaec8-9375-508f-8bec-bb1690e703a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186630875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cec30fa4-6553-5d60-9be2-7683bf1f3ab5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79340" :question/problem "Let $V$ be a $5\\times 9$ matrix that has $3$ pivot columns. What is the dimension of the null space of $V?$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\text{dim(Null}(V))$ is the number of non-pivot columns in $V.$ \n\nSince $V$ has $9$ columns and $3$ of them are pivots, then the number of non-pivot columns in $V$ is \n\n$$\n9-3=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{dim(Null}(V))=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186638248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21672c91-61dc-5836-a381-8c46717ca642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637794} {:answer/id #uuid "44472bc4-9ed5-50bc-99b0-4c9a35b213d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637852} {:answer/id #uuid "862761e1-1748-52bd-9e43-52d5f659e3ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637958} {:answer/id #uuid "8d85d242-753d-5368-b37c-f401149a45c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637968} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f95715-837d-5c81-a4dd-a93f30c5daf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186638108}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637968} :answer-field/id #uuid "246e2247-364f-5225-ac86-db66376a897e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186638158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47c65013-1732-5100-bc55-e9d107f3873c" :question/math-academy-id "q-73928" :question/problem "Let $r=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $-3r.$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying each component of $r$ by $-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3r & =-3\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\cdot (-2) \\\\ -3\\cdot 0 \\\\ -3\\cdot 1 \\\\ -3\\cdot (-6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186638280 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "110c14fb-6506-5cd8-9e84-ddaadce7c44d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 26 \\\\ -38 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637777} {:answer/id #uuid "1f37f90a-0dfe-5438-b5c0-0922ecae55d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 13 \\\\ -38 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637791} {:answer/id #uuid "a94d9705-c8b7-5e7a-9a66-478f16f6fcb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186638021} {:answer/id #uuid "b27d937d-aaff-5c33-8b8c-2e6a185a436e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{4} \\\\ \\frac{19}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186638032} {:answer/id #uuid "f97b8b16-97a3-5e2a-bc6e-4d611bf15536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186638131}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186638021} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e88c690-86aa-5166-9417-810793a9d063" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186638204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5addf14-a7f4-5eb8-9200-c4f6c0404c22" :question/math-academy-id "q-73934" :question/problem "Let $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 16 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $-\\frac{1}{4}v+2w.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply each component of $v$ by $-\\frac{1}{4}$ and each component of $w$ by $2.$ Then, we add the vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}v+2w & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 16 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+2 \\\\ -4+6 \\\\ 9-4 \\\\ -2+2 \\\\ 0+8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186649877 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8aa3d8f4-4dd2-506f-9d30-68ff62393a02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{A3},\\text{A2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649514} {:answer/id #uuid "a9da7786-867b-516e-88a7-76cfcc769559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{A3},\\text{A1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649576} {:answer/id #uuid "e8546719-3f35-5285-98d1-88344e941e88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{A2},\\text{A3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649676} {:answer/id #uuid "ec87bab0-f85f-50d8-a005-6e29ad4de517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{A2},\\text{A4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649680} {:answer/id #uuid "ff815307-a6a8-5e31-befd-31a3011a07c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{A1},\\text{A2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186649680} :answer-field/id #uuid "5691b35c-4791-523f-9cf8-2d9d32f1a0d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c9d790bb-c51c-5b6e-a080-62d1c64a64ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-94935" :question/problem "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:*\n\n*If $y$ is a vector such that $x+y=x$ for some $x\\in V$, then $y=0.$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+y)+(-x) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (y+x)+(-x) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A1}\\,\\,] \\\\ y+(x+(-x)) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,] \\\\ y+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,] \\\\ y & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+y)+(-x) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (y+x)+(-x) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A1}\\,] \\\\ y+(x+(-x)) & =x+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A2}\\,] \\\\ y+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A4}\\,] \\\\ y & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186649906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30c51f51-3aba-59da-a23e-7fc43ae1a697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\cdot f\\ne f$ for all $f\\in X$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649349} {:answer/id #uuid "33679cdb-3457-5da2-a5d5-71db6cf02737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "there exists a function $f\\in X$ and a scalar $c$ such that $cf\\notin X$, disproving closure under scalar multiplication." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649354} {:answer/id #uuid "44fb2310-9bb8-5131-8219-55448ce97dff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition of $f,g\\in X$ is not closed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649384} {:answer/id #uuid "9d03c7a2-04d1-57b5-bc1e-5ef2256b5c02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$X$ does not contain the zero element" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649555} {:answer/id #uuid "b13fdfc0-a6f9-5b4c-b237-168f914dcde1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "addition and scalar multiplication of functions in $X$ do not satisfy the distributive laws" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649588}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186649354} :answer-field/id #uuid "63c2943d-4035-5512-8146-b89dc747d702" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa85c6c1-7745-511d-8c4c-66cd60efb8cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-106815" :question/problem "Let $X$ be the set of functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in R.$ Complete the following sentence: \n\n*$X$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$X$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because **there exists a function $f\\in X$ and a scalar $c$ such that $cf\\notin X$, disproving closure under scalar multiplication.*** \n\nFor example, if $f(x)={x}^{2}+1,$ then $f(x)\\in X$ but  $-f(x)=-{x}^{2}-1\\notin X.$ Indeed, we have that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in R$ but $-f(x)=-{x}^{2}-1<0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of functions in $X$ is closed. Indeed, $(f+g)(x)=f(x)+g(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in R.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The zero function belongs to $X.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in X.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of functions satisfy the distributive laws. $\\,✓$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31d7f914-075a-518a-9203-a09f5028c555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650531} {:answer/id #uuid "349e9adc-a70c-580f-ab7f-7d0688cf986d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650533} {:answer/id #uuid "5f428b99-82e3-5ef1-8539-f14d92299849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650588} {:answer/id #uuid "60a4ca40-4b37-55e5-864c-bf60b6b2d712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650592} {:answer/id #uuid "87a840b5-61e3-57f1-869e-1b237f67d985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650669}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650592} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc30e5ab-81da-52a3-8fa9-10e0f3f8e345" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1a2f9b74-556d-5756-b9e3-d96eb609480f" :question/math-academy-id "q-111116" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 8 & -14 \\\\ 3 & -12 & 21\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $\\text{Null}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the equation $Ax=0.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 8 & -14 \\\\ 3 & -12 & 21\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -12 & 21\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is ${x}_{1}$, and we have two free variables, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ in terms of ${x}_{2}$  and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nFrom the first equation we get ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3},$ so the set of all the solutions of $Ax=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650946 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d57c202-4701-50c5-84c9-fdd4a038dc59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650518} {:answer/id #uuid "4e0149c1-e25a-5de9-bf49-75813ef6a6f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650566} {:answer/id #uuid "90dc442f-4243-572e-ba8d-bacd623e4f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650683} {:answer/id #uuid "e182a143-0bd4-525d-bc69-b0643d5187e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650800} {:answer/id #uuid "f8695c1a-4f3f-5311-90fd-b5646ced1d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650830}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650518} :answer-field/id #uuid "09ffe22a-8693-5c3c-8e97-55cfc35265f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a93d57c-06de-5c56-a47e-c0ed34543546" :question/math-academy-id "q-34979" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $b=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the value of $k$ such that $b\\in \\text{Null}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "If $b\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $Ab=0.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ab & =0 \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =0 \\\\ 5k-10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for this equation, we obtain $k=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07961243-89da-5e75-b204-fb7dac503762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650461} {:answer/id #uuid "6192094b-fbdc-596c-a148-9bca1276bd3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650595} {:answer/id #uuid "78c4928a-6c3c-5c19-b1bf-6a5863d11386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650633} {:answer/id #uuid "89c3b32f-18fa-57a5-bba4-3bf333e0e8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650672} {:answer/id #uuid "c32fe11f-8877-5931-b9d7-30122c58d96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650749}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650633} :answer-field/id #uuid "d549f045-e069-57c3-8cf3-8d6470d98576" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "968ba410-8b01-58b8-9569-8958f7c8095f" :question/math-academy-id "q-74868" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -10 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $b=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3k\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the value of $k$ such that $b\\in \\text{Null}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "If $b\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $Ab=0.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ab & =0 \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -10 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3k\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}30k+60 \\\\ -15k-30\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}30k+60 &=0 \\\\ -15k-30 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f4f9473-fc0a-5aed-a7fa-14d9fe67d2a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${b}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650546} {:answer/id #uuid "4c98dd5b-6614-5d7c-915c-bfc27caf59e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${b}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650563} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3acf34-e1a8-5390-a8c1-42a863bfe261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${b}_{2}$ and ${b}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650615} {:answer/id #uuid "83123bac-316b-5f1b-aa65-75c02efbd6be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${b}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650659} {:answer/id #uuid "8d422af6-7b08-52d8-b4bd-0f7b564ca011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650676}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650676} :answer-field/id #uuid "c14b15f2-3537-5e4f-bdd1-1d6fe4180ee4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c44c9302-8cce-5326-a685-fb92d76bb547" :question/math-academy-id "q-74862" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ and vectors ${b}_{1}$, ${b}_{2}$, ${b}_{3}$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\text{Null}(A)?$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A)$ if $Ab=0.$ So, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{b}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, none of the vectors lies in $\\text{Null}(A).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186653551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "271798a3-8708-57b9-8f96-c79d3d72ad3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 28\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653141} {:answer/id #uuid "3c835535-6174-59d7-b81d-cdca1b369670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653181} {:answer/id #uuid "91c12e11-967b-52e1-a914-215e37c1dc98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653288} {:answer/id #uuid "b7fb4215-76e7-5979-a334-e84bd46ae093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 28\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653339} {:answer/id #uuid "ee76b95b-0f83-5fa4-82e8-85a9b18670df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653426}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186653426} :answer-field/id #uuid "38c9bd4f-20f0-591a-bef6-bdb884079641" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54ea50ed-52ac-50cb-9192-fc5157abf24a" :question/math-academy-id "q-111194" :question/problem "Consider the matrix \n$D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 15 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 28\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(D).$" :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(D),$ we reduce the matrix $D$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 15 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 28\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 28\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 28 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${d}_{1}$ and ${d}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$ is $\\{{d}_{1},{d}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186662269 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e0bc9d0-2e68-5cde-863e-5434a0ffd6aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661668} {:answer/id #uuid "3b719b56-5d22-5c11-af1d-d7f20c4b2e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661733} {:answer/id #uuid "6f12e882-d5a7-5c8b-95d3-e6be917f8987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R∖\\{0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661818} {:answer/id #uuid "ac55e5a5-9301-58a1-a572-537d75cb4102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661926} {:answer/id #uuid "ed6625cf-1d79-5017-b7d5-76246a36da48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Col}(A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186662037}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186661733} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac7457d2-9897-5202-8cd3-a4f2ae17d8fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186662196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30d82a21-5442-5cf4-8415-ea5458f19487" :question/math-academy-id "q-141346" :question/problem "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border:2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\{0\\}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186662306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "316c8128-bf1b-594d-aa87-80d2405d4d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661705} {:answer/id #uuid "79e7db3d-0c7c-52d4-865d-08d1738f692e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Col}(A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661841} {:answer/id #uuid "8691a21c-d9d3-543a-9822-8586bec18da0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661849} {:answer/id #uuid "98fdde56-793b-5a01-8c6e-d55a7b78d624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{rank}(A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661890} {:answer/id #uuid "ca47264a-e0c0-56eb-9aef-b016124a1fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661971}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186661971} :answer-field/id #uuid "f60205cc-6828-5804-8b00-80e0ebeba009" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186662248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7849c7a6-8771-5340-9ff8-55577a1a84b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-141347" :question/problem "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=\\bbox[3pt,white,border:2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}}=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186662343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bb0df87-6b66-5944-b1dd-454ba552f9db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661659} {:answer/id #uuid "228b7b0a-3160-5b2a-b1d1-61d079480a0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661678} {:answer/id #uuid "314661e8-5e45-5943-b8e5-9fcd7ab80791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661704} {:answer/id #uuid "34d03e7e-d18b-5ca3-92b3-37a181e42b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661714} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9f293d-624e-5ed9-913e-8cb4be3d342c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661762}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186661659} :answer-field/id #uuid "f75f7499-83c9-59af-ba8f-207dccb35553" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186662249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dae4fbb5-bcbf-541b-9afd-ea52e3dddf91" :question/math-academy-id "q-47604" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{rank}(A)=n$\n\nII. $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0$\n\nIII. $A$ has $n$ pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ then  $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$* $\\,\\times$\n\nSince $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ then we have $\\text{rank}(A)=\\text{dim(Col}(A))\\le n-1\\ne n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ then  $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0.$* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, by the invertible matrix theorem, if  $\\text{Col}(A)$ is not ${R}^{n}$ then  $A$ is not an invertible matrix and  so we must have $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ then  $A$ has $n$ pivot columns.* $\\,\\times$  \n\nSince $\\text{Col}(A)⊂{R}^{n},$ the matrix $A$ can have at most  $n-1$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186662344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "410d25ee-90ba-563c-943d-db4052119c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661739} {:answer/id #uuid "946260af-9902-5d47-9925-fbe161873cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661880} {:answer/id #uuid "9d18bcd5-71b2-5b84-bee1-7f8b1aefb062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661898} {:answer/id #uuid "c6f069db-9ecb-576b-a1d9-18ecf527a428" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661962} {:answer/id #uuid "cb788b15-b090-5bb3-9a67-25e175ed8608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661974}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186661962} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1fe6272-f996-503c-bc14-a61bb015472a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186662247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db5a5ae3-0add-50b7-955f-ff73e4e003cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-141214" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{Null}(A)$ has no basis\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{n}$\n\nIII. $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0$" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Null}(A)$ has no basis.* $\\,\\times$  \n\nSince $det(A)=0,$ it must be $Ax=0$ has non-trivial solution, which means $\\text{Null}(A)$ has basis.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{n}.$*$\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, $A$ is not invertible because $det(A)=0.$  Since $A$ is not invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, it must be $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{n}.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0.$*  $\\,\\times$\n\nSince $det(A)=0,$ then $Ax=0$ has non-trivial solution, which means that $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186666079 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b56df73-aa46-5f4e-a505-d3ce57b2cfc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186665531} {:answer/id #uuid "131b3cd7-dc78-553f-97e8-18aadee35948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186665544} {:answer/id #uuid "68519536-a6a2-546e-a4b6-fe69fec7b8fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186665672} {:answer/id #uuid "74951e8c-9bf2-5933-8328-76f20c61c382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186665696} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6578ee-1c85-581c-a0ba-85eb3b4d7045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186665714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186665531} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6be0fb8-bc1a-52e2-ac2b-39e8ff3d07a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186666052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33dac1e0-5010-5398-bdf2-b11f1ab161aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-111180" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(V).$" :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(V),$ we reduce the matrix $V$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186666147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "038757a5-657c-5b6d-82f0-b78ce5bc4777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186665513} {:answer/id #uuid "347fef0d-def7-5bf6-ad8e-85abc842f21d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186665596} {:answer/id #uuid "396f6fc5-8f79-5b4f-889f-62e070e11e1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186665608} {:answer/id #uuid "5cb5bcd3-4a21-5ff5-b21c-8a455e682489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186665654} {:answer/id #uuid "daf1a8b3-9670-5ce3-8344-a1907609a3ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186665858}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186665596} :answer-field/id #uuid "be986690-061b-5c5f-ad1a-d859a6f86b79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186666024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8bc313e-801c-544d-ac27-64f93c68c0c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-34527" :question/problem "Consider the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle H=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 9 & -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \nFind a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(H).$" :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(H),$ we reduce the matrix $H$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 9 & -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 9 & -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(H)$ is $\\{{h}_{1},{h}_{3}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673117 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15b93aa7-c591-5841-aa3a-357d8bf16eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673116} {:answer/id #uuid "45cde434-4d75-5c09-9b0f-35b920274076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673121} {:answer/id #uuid "52405711-96a4-58b9-b20b-772647cc4c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673122} {:answer/id #uuid "70a7bdca-b802-5569-bfd3-d23438559962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673127} {:answer/id #uuid "cddf6d9c-c0bf-57b1-b126-0839ec3bed59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673144}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673116} :answer-field/id #uuid "e61f64c2-6321-5d18-9115-5e8492b4d1ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24f7a599-64fa-59b0-bc94-5119ac1b11b5" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\operatorname{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$.*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{Null}(A)$ has no basis\n\nII. $\\operatorname{Col}(A)\\subsetneq\\mathbb{R}^{n}$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)=0$" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}A\\text{ is invertible}\\\\\\Updownarrow\\\\\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A)&=\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\\\\\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A))&=n=\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\\\\\operatorname{Null}(A)&=\\{0\\}\\\\\\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A))&=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\operatorname{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}$, then $\\operatorname{Null}(A)$ has no basis.* ×\n\nEvery finite-dimensional subspace has a basis. The zero subspace has the empty basis.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\operatorname{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}$, then $\\operatorname{Col}(A)\\subsetneq\\mathbb{R}^{n}$.* ×\n\nThe given condition implies $\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n$ and $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)=0$. Thus $\\operatorname{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^{n}$.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\operatorname{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}$, then $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)=0$.* ✓\n\nThe given condition implies $\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n$ and $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)=0$. Thus $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is III only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e0c05b-14aa-5e14-b955-527ebc07ec4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673113} {:answer/id #uuid "3b0d39f6-4254-5d22-bf4e-6febe4ad2533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673119}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673119} :answer-field/id #uuid "956051b9-5768-5547-88b9-3b7e942076a5" :answer-field/key "$\\det(A)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673134} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "348adaf6-7787-565e-9ff4-1f701aa97384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673118} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5e628e-f4dc-5d13-9581-7e8bb52ecd66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673147}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673118} :answer-field/id #uuid "987163a9-96d8-5ed7-b484-24cd137c96e9" :answer-field/key "$\\operatorname{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^{7}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673137} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14c0ef31-2044-5855-a7a9-cab2011298ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673115} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a583d1-5c19-57bb-b0b1-2de813730ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673140}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673115} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0d31553-b93e-5bf7-a7e6-04e42a08a1de" :answer-field/key "$A$ has fewer than $7$ pivot columns" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "635f4b7d-7d59-52ae-8c0d-6343e80d6951" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)=0$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$.*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}A\\text{ is invertible}\\\\\\Updownarrow\\\\\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A)&=\\mathbb{R}^{7}\\\\\\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A))&=7=\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\\\\\operatorname{Null}(A)&=\\{0\\}\\\\\\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A))&=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)=0$, then $\\det(A)=0$.* ×\n\nThe given condition implies $\\operatorname{rank}(A)=7$ and $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)=0$. Thus $\\det(A)\\ne 0$.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)=0$, then $\\operatorname{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^{7}$.* ✓\n\nThe given condition implies $\\operatorname{rank}(A)=7$ and $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)=0$. Thus $\\operatorname{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^{7}$.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)=0$, then $A$ has fewer than $7$ pivot columns.* ×\n\nThe given condition implies $\\operatorname{rank}(A)=7$ and $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)=0$. Thus $A$ has $7$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\operatorname{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^{7}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673141 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81068501-c0e2-5f32-acb0-2aace6ed6c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673132} {:answer/id #uuid "99a5c457-06a2-52cc-aa45-339994a36685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673138}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673132} :answer-field/id #uuid "01abd1c7-c59f-5c99-9277-f9537d6b88ca" :answer-field/key "$A$ has $n$ pivot columns" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673111} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d553a7c-18de-5cec-850b-80701a20b3ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673123} {:answer/id #uuid "9001b869-f87a-5bff-81cb-628feb2d2463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673133}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673123} :answer-field/id #uuid "752229c8-1a98-538b-b81c-a0f7d16e0605" :answer-field/key "$\\operatorname{rank}(A)<n$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673129} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1045ca11-00a7-54f5-82df-d90677220306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673114} {:answer/id #uuid "987039ff-1afd-51f5-acdd-306be4aaac88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673136}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673136} :answer-field/id #uuid "970549a2-6eb7-57d6-af88-f32a52d1299f" :answer-field/key "$A$ is **not** invertible" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a51a92b7-4da1-53a7-a184-875247f5a8f5" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\operatorname{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$.*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}A\\text{ is invertible}\\\\\\Updownarrow\\\\\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A)&=\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\\\\\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A))&=n=\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\\\\\operatorname{Null}(A)&=\\{0\\}\\\\\\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A))&=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\operatorname{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, then $A$ is **not** invertible.* ×\n\nThe given condition implies $\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n$ and $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)=0$. Thus $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\operatorname{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, then $\\operatorname{rank}(A)<n$.* ×\n\nThe given condition implies $\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n$ and $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)=0$. Thus $\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n$.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\operatorname{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, then $A$ has $n$ pivot columns.* ✓\n\nThe given condition implies $\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n$ and $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)=0$. Thus $A$ has $n$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $A$ has $n$ pivot columns."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673148 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040ad0ff-3100-5425-b2f1-da7e669a2b4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673112} {:answer/id #uuid "4238513f-e322-530e-9565-e1ef6525b9d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673120} {:answer/id #uuid "756e0384-4d12-5af5-b55c-3d54c0e1e087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673130} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c55ca2-2506-540d-8b37-d903917854aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673142} {:answer/id #uuid "f803c880-b273-55c6-86f9-5f9ae0631781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673120} :answer-field/id #uuid "64adbc10-22f8-5b06-93cc-be533f313081" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ece9d320-1fda-5bca-bfdf-852dee2b03f3" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\operatorname{Null}(A)\\ne\\{0\\}$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$.*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $A$ is **not** invertible\n\nII. $\\det(A)=0$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n$" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}A\\text{ is invertible}\\\\\\Updownarrow\\\\\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A)&=\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\\\\\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A))&=n=\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\\\\\operatorname{Null}(A)&=\\{0\\}\\\\\\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A))&=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\operatorname{Null}(A)\\ne\\{0\\}$, then $A$ is **not** invertible.* ✓\n\nThe given condition implies $\\operatorname{rank}(A)<n$ and $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)>0$. Thus $A$ is **not** invertible.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\operatorname{Null}(A)\\ne\\{0\\}$, then $\\det(A)=0$.* ✓\n\nThe given condition implies $\\operatorname{rank}(A)<n$ and $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)>0$. Thus $\\det(A)=0$.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\operatorname{Null}(A)\\ne\\{0\\}$, then $\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n$.* ×\n\nThe given condition implies $\\operatorname{rank}(A)<n$ and $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)>0$. Thus $\\operatorname{rank}(A)<n$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is I and II only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673150 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cf403e2-fc73-51e8-93ac-21bd4fa4deb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673126} {:answer/id #uuid "735ac19d-aa52-5baf-a48d-1b36bd66de03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673128} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa16602-e7c9-54cd-a27f-713bd6b96dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673131} {:answer/id #uuid "9b066337-30b7-5051-a050-f4cc7caba6b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673139} {:answer/id #uuid "f20ce81b-8827-55b8-8eda-03664eee7b4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673149}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673149} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7a0c1cc-ebfa-5947-959b-5472ae9f3909" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f57a7c6f-101e-5b33-aef0-c7309a1bc9ae" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\operatorname{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$.*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{rank}(A)<n$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)>0$\n\nIII. $A$ has $n$ pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}A\\text{ is invertible}\\\\\\Updownarrow\\\\\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A)&=\\mathbb{R}^{n}\\\\\\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A))&=n=\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\\\\\operatorname{Null}(A)&=\\{0\\}\\\\\\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A))&=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\operatorname{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, then $\\operatorname{rank}(A)<n$.* ×\n\nThe given condition implies $\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n$ and $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)=0$. Thus $\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n$.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\operatorname{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, then $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)>0$.* ×\n\nThe given condition implies $\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n$ and $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)=0$. Thus $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)=0$.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\operatorname{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, then $A$ has $n$ pivot columns.* ✓\n\nThe given condition implies $\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n$ and $\\operatorname{nullity}(A)=0$. Thus $A$ has $n$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is III only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673154 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c4b21a8-5857-5ef8-bfd3-a65eb1a523c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673165} {:answer/id #uuid "9a973bcf-70db-5a89-a689-b55ce1af4a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mathbb{R}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673172} {:answer/id #uuid "b1783284-0306-56fc-9b19-67e44e5dc329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673175} {:answer/id #uuid "b2583ecf-8bbb-5a3a-9b7b-8101ae883bee" :an